 `tools/parity.mjs`, `tools/reference-check.mjs`.  Read first: `src/framework/contracts/README.md`, `src/framework/ir/README.md`, `src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts`, `src/typed/strategies.ts`, `src/typed/passes.ts`, and the tickets under `ttmp/2026/11/24/` to `ttmp/2026/11/26/` that your task names.  Your ticket is IRWB-035-B. This is a **research and implementation stage**, in one session of up to three hours. Write a design first and commit it. Then implement, test, measure, and write a final report in the ticket.  ## Your task (A0): what share of the optimizer's edits are local value rewrites? Research programme A rests on a hypothesis nobody has measured: if most of the value edits O2 and `speed` make are local rewrites (a small expression replaced by an equal one), a function's equivalence could be assembled from per-rule proofs instead of a sampled meaning check (`ttmp/2026/11/25/IRWB-006--...`, `ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-008--...` and `IRWB-010--...` describe verified rules and the exact checks built on them).  - **Classify every edit** that the O2 and `speed` pipelines make on the corpus, using the correspondence and the edit records the framework keeps:   - **local rewrite:** a value replaced by an equal one, citing or matching a rule;   - **structural:** control flow, loop-invariant motion, inlining, unrolling, register allocation;   - **other.**    Define each class precisely enough that the classification is reproducible, and say how you decide the borderline cases. - **The share** per pass, per pipeline and per project, by edit count and by the instructions the edits affect. - **Of the local rewrites,** what share already cites a verified rule, and which rules or rule shapes would cover most of the rest. - **The classifier is a tool,** callable by other code, with a test, so later stages can rerun it.  ## Measure and report - the classification method and its definitions; - the tables: per pass, per pipeline, per project; - the verified-rule coverage of the local rewrites, and the rules that would raise it most; - what the numbers mean for programme A (proof by rules instead of sampling), with the limits of the measurement; - the framework changes, if any, each with its reason.  ## Repository facts - **Build and test:** build with `npm run build` (`tsc`, into `dist/`). Tests are `node --test tests/<dir>/<name>.test.mjs` and import from `../../dist/...`; on Node 24, pass files, not a bare directory. Run `tests/typed/*.test.mjs`, `tests/framework/*.test.mjs` and `tests/workbench/*.test.mjs` before your last commit. Do not run the full suite (`npm test`): other agents share this machine. Prettier: 110 columns, single quotes; run it only on files that were formatted before you changed them. If you change anything under `src/framework/` or `src/workbench/`, also check that `node tools/build-site.mjs` still builds (the browser bundle cannot import Node built-ins). - **Scope:** you may change `src/typed/`, `tests/typed/`, `tools/` where the task needs it, and `src/framework/` where the framework does not fit, with each change and its reason recorded as a primary result. Do not change `src/tensor/`, the corpus's programs or their expected results, unless your task says so. - **Scripts:** put every probe, benchmark and temporary program you write in your ticket's `scripts/` directory, numbered in the order you write them (`01-name.mjs`), and commit them. Nothing you ran should exist only in a scratch directory or a one-off command. - **Sandboxed sessions:** a test that spawns `node` can fail with EPERM. Request escalation for that command. - **Tickets and commits:** use your docmgr and diary skills for the ticket and diary. Commit on your current branch with messages starting `IRWB-035-B:`, as you go, so that your last commit always holds working code. Do not push. - **Outside your worktree,** use nothing.  ## Final message - the ticket, design and report paths; - the headline shares (local, structural, other) for O2 and `speed`; - the verified-rule coverage of local rewrites; - the framework changes; - the last commit hash.
