*research and implementation stage**, in one session of up to three hours. Write a design first and commit it. Then implement, test, measure, and write a final report in the ticket.  ## Your task (B1): specialize the bytecode VM on its bytecode, a first Futamura projection The bytecode VM (`corpus/projects/bytecode-vm/`, ticket `ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-012--bytecode-vm/`) runs a bytecode program from a buffer. Hand-specialized loops for its two programs take 75 and 74 instructions, against 7,678 and 6,874 for the general VM. The existing specialization (IRWB-009 and IRWB-010, `src/typed/specialize.ts`) binds scalar values and fields, not memory, and binding scalars here gains almost nothing.  - **Specialize on a constant buffer:** a host promise that a buffer's contents are fixed, checked as the field binding is checked, and a binding-time analysis that propagates it through loads from that buffer. - **Specialize the dispatch loop** on a known program counter: when to unroll it, how to stop, and how the result stays checked by the meaning contract. - **Measure how much of the gap** to the hand-specialized 75 and 74 instructions automatic specialization closes, and what it costs in code size. - **New approaches are welcome,** for example polyvariant specialization with a size budget, or a check that the promise holds at run time. Report what worked and what did not.  ## Measure and report - the binding-time analysis and the dispatch-loop specialization, with their checks; - instructions and code size of the specialized VM for both programs, against the general VM and the hand-specialized loops; - what stops the specialization, and where it falls short; - the framework changes, each with its reason; - which results you measured and which you only argued.  ## Repository facts - **Build and test:** build with `npm run build` (`tsc`, into `dist/`). Tests are `node --test tests/<dir>/<name>.test.mjs` and import from `../../dist/...`; on Node 24, pass files, not a bare directory. Run `tests/typed/*.test.mjs`, `tests/framework/*.test.mjs` and `tests/workbench/*.test.mjs` before your last commit. Do not run the full suite (`npm test`): other agents share this machine. Prettier: 110 columns, single quotes; run it only on files that were formatted before you changed them. If you change anything under `src/framework/` or `src/workbench/`, also check that `node tools/build-site.mjs` still builds (the browser bundle cannot import Node built-ins). - **Scope:** you may change `src/typed/`, `tests/typed/`, `tools/` where the task needs it, and `src/framework/` where the framework does not fit, with each change and its reason recorded as a primary result. Do not change `src/tensor/`, the corpus's programs or their expected results, unless your task says so. - **Scripts:** put every probe, benchmark and temporary program you write in your ticket's `scripts/` directory, numbered in the order you write them (`01-name.mjs`), and commit them. Nothing you ran should exist only in a scratch directory or a one-off command. - **Sandboxed sessions:** a test that spawns `node` can fail with EPERM. Request escalation for that command. - **Tickets and commits:** use your docmgr and diary skills for the ticket and diary. Commit on your current branch with messages starting `IRWB-036-E:`, as you go, so that your last commit always holds working code. Do not push. - **Outside your worktree,** use nothing.  ## Final message - the ticket, design and report paths; - the instructions and code size for both programs: general, specialized, hand-written; - the framework changes; - the last commit hash.
