assification method and its definitions; - the tables: per pass, per pipeline, per project; - the verified-rule coverage of the local rewrites, and the rules that would raise it most; - what the numbers mean for programme A (proof by rules instead of sampling), with the limits of the measurement; - the framework changes, if any, each with its reason.  ## Repository facts - **Build and test:** build with `npm run build` (`tsc`, into `dist/`). Tests are `node --test tests/<dir>/<name>.test.mjs` and import from `../../dist/...`; on Node 24, pass files, not a bare directory. Run `tests/typed/*.test.mjs`, `tests/framework/*.test.mjs` and `tests/workbench/*.test.mjs` before your last commit. Do not run the full suite (`npm test`): other agents share this machine. Prettier: 110 columns, single quotes; run it only on files that were formatted before you changed them. If you change anything under `src/framework/` or `src/workbench/`, also check that `node tools/build-site.mjs` still builds (the browser bundle cannot import Node built-ins). - **Scope:** you may change `src/typed/`, `tests/typed/`, `tools/` where the task needs it, and `src/framework/` where the framework does not fit, with each change and its reason recorded as a primary result. Do not change `src/tensor/`, the corpus's programs or their expected results, unless your task says so. - **Scripts:** put every probe, benchmark and temporary program you write in your ticket's `scripts/` directory, numbered in the order you write them (`01-name.mjs`), and commit them. Nothing you ran should exist only in a scratch directory or a one-off command. - **Sandboxed sessions:** a test that spawns `node` can fail with EPERM. Request escalation for that command. - **Tickets and commits:** use your docmgr and diary skills for the ticket and diary. Commit on your current branch with messages starting `IRWB-035-C:`, as you go, so that your last commit always holds working code. Do not push. - **Outside your worktree,** use nothing.  ## Final message - the ticket, design and report paths; - the headline shares (local, structural, other) for O2 and `speed`; - the verified-rule coverage of local rewrites; - the framework changes; - the last commit hash.
